-- `Chunked` and `Contiguous` - Implementation-Containers.md §5.
--
-- Contiguity is the one-chunk case of a more general property. `Chunked` is what a function that
-- only reads elements asks for: it accepts every container, and the push-style iterator lowering is
-- what makes it free - a contiguous container yields one slice and fuses into the loop it compiles
-- to today, while a chunked one becomes a nested loop whose inner loop runs at full contiguous
-- speed. `Contiguous` is the stronger promise, about a buffer address, and it is what `[a]` means.
--
-- Both classes and both instances for `Array` live in Collections; what this fixture adds is a
-- container of its own on each side of the line.

-- The chunked half: three fixed-size chunks with a count of how many are in use, which is
-- §13.1's worked example reduced to the part that iterates. Nothing here is contiguous - the
-- elements live in three separate runs - and that is the whole point.
data Chunks {a: [Int], b: [Int], c: [Int], used: Int}

fn threeChunks(x: Int) -> Chunks =
    Chunks {a: [x, x + 1], b: [x + 2, x + 3], c: [x + 4, x + 5], used: 3}

{-
   The instance, and the one thing to notice about it: `chunks` is an `iter fn`, so a class member
   may be iterator-shaped and each `yield` hands over a *view* of one chunk. The class declares the
   written shape - what an instance takes and what it hands over - and the continuation parameter is
   synthesized here, in the implementation, which is where the `yield` is.

   Three `yield`s rather than a loop over an array of chunks, because `[[Int]]` would need the
   element teardown an array of arrays still waits on. The shape a caller sees is the same.
-}
instance Chunked(Chunks, Int):
    iter fn chunks(self: Chunks) -> '[Int]:
        yield self.a
        if self.used > 1:
            yield self.b
        if self.used > 2:
            yield self.c

-- The contiguous half: a container of the program's own that promises a buffer address. One
-- instance, one line, and it is what makes `total(ring)` below compile - `[Int]` is what `elements`
-- answers, so passing this where a slice is expected is the ordinary conversion rather than a copy.
data Ring {items: [Int]}

instance Contiguous(Ring, Int):
    fn elements(return self: Ring) -> '[Int] = elements(self.items)

instance Chunked(Ring, Int):
    iter fn chunks(self: Ring) -> '[Int] = yield elements(self)

-- The element iterator every loop below goes through once it has a chunk. Written over `[a]`,
-- which is what a chunk is.
iter fn each(xs: [a]) -> a:
    let &i = 0 :: Int
    let n = truncate(length(xs)) :: Int

    while i < n:
        yield xs[i]
        i = i + 1

{-
   The nested loop, which is what `Chunked` is for.

   `for chunk in chunks(xs)` reaches a class function by name and the instance decides which body
   runs; after specialization there is no dispatch left in either loop - the outer one is a direct
   call to the instance's `chunks` and the inner one is the same subscript loop a `[Int]` has always
   compiled to.
-}
fn sumChunked(xs: Chunks) -> Int:
    let &total = 0 :: Int

    for chunk in chunks(xs):
        for x in each(chunk):
            total = total + x

    return total

-- The same loop over the contiguous container, which yields exactly one chunk. Written identically,
-- which is the property §5 wants: the caller does not know or care how many chunks there were.
fn sumContiguous(xs: Ring) -> Int:
    let &total = 0 :: Int

    for chunk in chunks(xs):
        for x in each(chunk):
            total = total + x

    return total

-- And over the library's own array, whose `Chunked` instance is Collections'.
fn sumArray(xs: Array(Int)) -> Int:
    let &total = 0 :: Int

    for chunk in chunks(xs):
        for x in each(chunk):
            total = total + x

    return total

-- A contiguous container of the program's own, passed where `[Int]` was expected. No conversion is
-- written and none is a copy: `elements` hands back a view rooted in the argument.
fn total(xs: [Int]) -> Int:
    let &sum = 0 :: Int

    for x in each(xs):
        sum = sum + x

    return sum

-- The same, through a signature written over a type variable, since the two bind differently: a
-- concrete `[Int]` parameter reaches the conversion at the argument, and a `[a]` one has to bind `a`
-- from the container before there is anything to convert.
fn count(xs: [a]) -> Int = truncate(length(xs)) :: Int

-- A loop that leaves through both frames. The `return` travels out of the inner iterator's step
-- signal and then out of the outer one's, which is the same path a nested `for` over two plain
-- iterators takes - the class in front of the outer one changes nothing about it.
fn firstOver(xs: Chunks, bound: Int) -> Int:
    for chunk in chunks(xs):
        for x in each(chunk):
            if x > bound:
                return x

    return -1

-- `break` in the inner loop ends that chunk and not the walk, which is what makes the two loops
-- independent: the outer one goes on to the next chunk.
fn firstOfEachChunk(xs: Chunks) -> Int:
    let &total = 0 :: Int

    for chunk in chunks(xs):
        for x in each(chunk):
            total = total + x
            break

    return total

fn main() -> Int:
    let cs = threeChunks(1)
    let ring = Ring {items: [10, 20, 30]}
    let arr = [4, 5, 6]

    return sumChunked(cs) +          -- 1+2+3+4+5+6 = 21
           sumContiguous(ring) +     -- 60
           sumArray(arr) +           -- 15
           total(ring) +             -- 60
           count(ring) +             -- 3
           firstOver(cs, 3) +        -- 4
           firstOfEachChunk(cs)      -- 1 + 3 + 5 = 9
